.docname {How it runs}
.include {docs}

This page shows vCortex while it works: who talks to whom, in which order, and what data moves
between them. It stays at the big picture; later pages open each piece.

## The big picture

Three places take part: the **robot**, the **backend** on a laptop, and the **browser**.

.mermaid caption:{The data path, from the lidar to the screen.}
    flowchart TB
        subgraph Robot
            direction LR
            L[Lidar and IMU] --> SLAM[Point-LIO<br/>builds the map]
            SLAM --> MB[map_builder<br/>one dot per 5 cm cube]
            MB --> BR[bridge]
        end
        subgraph Backend
            direction LR
            ST[(Map store)]
            WK[Model worker<br/>every 3 s]
            MO[Model]
            CO[Connectors<br/>JSON · DXF · PLY]
            ST --> WK --> MO --> CO
        end
        subgraph Browser
            direction LR
            LV[Left view<br/>raw dots]
            RV[Right view<br/>digital twin]
        end
        FILE[/Saved map<br/>offline mode/] --> ST
        BR -- "/ingest" --> ST
        ST -- "/ws/raw" --> LV
        CO -- "/ws/twin" --> RV

- The robot keeps its own software to itself. A small **bridge** sends the map out over one
  connection, `/ingest`.
- The backend keeps a copy of the map in the **map store**. Every 3 seconds, if the map changed,
  the **model worker** runs the model on a copy and writes the result in every format.
- The browser gets two live streams: the **raw dots** (`/ws/raw`) and the **twin** in the chosen
  format (`/ws/twin`).
- Without a robot, a **saved map** fills the map store instead.

## Clean Architecture

The backend follows **Clean Architecture**: the important logic sits in the middle and knows
nothing about the outside world. Each ring may use the rings inside it, never the ones outside.

![Clean Architecture rings](images/clean-architecture.svg "The backend as rings: each ring uses only what is inside it.")

| Ring | Folders | Libraries it may use |
|---|---|---|
| Domain | `domain/` | The C++ standard library, Eigen for vectors |
| Use cases | `model/`, `geometry/` | PCL and Eigen, as math libraries |
| Adapters | `connectors/`, `protocol/` | nlohmann/json |
| Frameworks and drivers | `io/`, `main.cpp` | Crow (web server), threads, files |

The model is **pure**: it gets a map, a path and settings, and returns a building. It never reads
a file or the network. So the live robot, a saved map and the tests all run the very same code.

## A live session, step by step

### 1. The robot joins

.mermaid caption:{A source connects and introduces itself.}
    sequenceDiagram
        participant R as Robot bridge
        participant S as Backend server
        participant M as Map store
        participant W as Model worker
        participant B as Browser (twin view)
        R->>S: open /ingest
        R->>S: hello (protocol 2, token, frame name, cube size)
        alt wrong protocol or token
            S-->>R: close
        else accepted
            S->>M: reset: forget the old map
            S->>W: clear: forget the old twin
            S-->>B: empty message: drop the old twin
            S-->>R: welcome
        end

Only one source feeds the backend at a time. A newer source replaces the old one: a robot that
lost Wi-Fi never says goodbye, and it must not lock everyone else out.

### 2. The map streams in

.mermaid caption:{Map pieces arrive and go straight on to the left view.}
    sequenceDiagram
        participant R as Robot bridge
        participant S as Backend server
        participant M as Map store
        participant V as Browser (raw view)
        R->>S: whole map (all cubes)
        S->>M: store every cube
        S-->>V: whole map
        loop while the robot walks
            R->>S: delta (new and changed cubes)
            alt next in order
                S->>M: add or replace those cubes
                S-->>V: the same cubes
            else a delta went missing
                S-->>R: resync: send the whole map again
            end
            R->>S: poses (where the robot is)
            S->>M: add to the path
        end

Each cube travels as 20 bytes: its position, its brightness and how many laser turns saw it.
A viewer on a slow connection skips some deltas and later gets the whole map at once, so it never
falls behind for good.

### 3. The model runs

.mermaid caption:{Every few seconds, the worker turns the latest map into a twin.}
    sequenceDiagram
        participant W as Model worker
        participant M as Map store
        participant MO as Model
        participant C as Connectors
        participant B as Browser (twin view)
        loop every 3 s, or at once after new settings
            W->>M: take a copy, if the map changed
            M-->>W: dots and path
            W->>MO: run the model
            MO-->>W: building and report
            W->>C: write JSON, DXF and PLY
            C-->>W: one file per format
            W-->>B: the file in the format each viewer chose
        end

The worker copies the map and runs on the copy, so new map pieces keep arriving while the model
works. If many deltas arrive in 3 seconds, they cost one model run, not many.

### 4. The person at the screen

.mermaid caption:{What the browser asks for, and when.}
    sequenceDiagram
        actor U as You
        participant B as Browser
        participant S as Backend server
        participant W as Model worker
        U->>B: open the page
        B->>S: formats, settings, status
        B->>S: open /ws/raw and /ws/twin?format=twin-json
        S-->>B: the current map and the latest twin
        loop every 2 s
            B->>S: status (points, last run, what was found)
        end
        U->>B: pick another format
        B->>S: close the twin stream, open it again with the new format
        U->>B: type a wall height
        B->>S: new settings
        S->>W: new settings: run now
        W-->>B: a new twin
        U->>B: export
        B->>S: download the latest twin file

## Offline mode

With `--map`, the backend opens a saved map at start and needs no robot.

.mermaid caption:{Offline mode: a saved map takes the robot's place.}
    sequenceDiagram
        participant F as Saved map
        participant S as Backend
        participant M as Map store
        participant W as Model worker
        participant R as Robot bridge
        S->>F: read the map and the path beside it
        S->>M: store them, once
        W->>M: take a copy
        W->>W: run the model, once
        R->>S: open /ingest
        S-->>R: close: offline mode

After that, the browser works just like in a live session. The map never changes, so the model
runs again only when you change a setting.

## Inside one model run

One run follows the same steps every time. Each step takes what the one before it found.

.mermaid caption:{The steps of one model run, with what passes between them.}
    flowchart TD
        IN[/Dots and path/] --> CL[Clean<br/>drop stray dots]
        CL --> FL[Floor<br/>find the floor plane]
        FL --> AL[Align<br/>turn the map square]
        AL --> FS[Free floor<br/>where the robot could walk, split at doors]
        FS --> OU[Outlines<br/>walk out of each room to the wall faces]
        OU --> WA[Walls<br/>faces become walls]
        WA --> OB[Objects<br/>leftover dots become boxes]
        WA --> RO[Rooms<br/>the space the walls close]
        OB --> BACK[Turn back<br/>to the map's own angle]
        RO --> BACK
        BACK --> OUT[/Building: walls, rooms, objects/]

| Step | Real numbers, from the room in the [Overview](introduction.qd) |
|---|---|
| Dots in | 192,149 |
| After cleaning | 186,427 (97%) |
| Floor tilt | 2.4° |
| Turn to square the map | 16.8° |
| Found | 4 walls, 1 room, 19 objects |
| Time | about 1.5 s |

The [Algorithm](algorithm.qd) section explains each step in detail.
